ACTUAL RENDERED ENGLISH NARRATIVE

Start from the point where the virtual capture was blocked, and follow the path from the completion notification to frame processing.



Once the allocator was prepared, the official cam capture completed.


In this baseline reproduction, the default WSL kernel did not expose a DMA-BUF allocator, so the official cam could not allocate buffers.

The unmodified official cam (source commit 293d751a) was run independently three times, in three separate processes, on the recorded WSL2 trial kernel with the Virtual2 400x300 NV12 virtual camera configuration. In each run, normal completion of three frames and successful udmabuf allocation were observed. CPU-access success from the separate DMA-BUF probe was recorded separately from this cam allocation observation. The trial ended with the default kernel restored, so this configuration cannot be rerun as-is in the current state.


The virtual pipeline produces video and notifies request completion.

In the reference source, the official libcamera virtual feature supplies fake external camera frames instead of a real sensor, and those frames are either software-generated test patterns or configured .jpg image files. The provided Virtual2 configuration uses a 400x300 resolution with the lines test pattern, which is diagonal stripes.


From pattern creation through cam's completion notification


Mermaid source




Virtual video also requires actual buffers: through Camera::exportFrameBuffers, PipelineHandlerVirtual::exportFrameBuffers allocates DMA-BUF buffers, and cam attaches them to Request. On the selected NV12 path, TestPatternGenerator::generateFrame uses a write-direction DmaSyncer and a write mapping. After that, the CPU converts the pattern to NV12 and writes it into the buffer.

On the virtual video path, requests are delivered asynchronously, and VirtualCameraData::processRequest waits until the target time before attempting to write the test pattern. If generation fails, the request is marked as FrameError, and success is not asserted from the buffer completion signal alone. completeBuffer proceeds to completeRequest only when it returns completion for all buffers, and a completion request whose turn has come is delivered to the cam callback connected to the requestCompleted signal. If an earlier request is still incomplete, notification of the later request is held.


cam receives the completion notification and processes the frame in the event loop.


The completion notification (requestComplete) is a separate step from follow-up frame handling in the camera session (processRequest). requestComplete returns immediately if the request status is Request::RequestCancelled; otherwise, it defers processRequest to the event loop using callLater. The comment states that this deferral is meant to avoid blocking the camera manager thread.


From completion notification to subsequent processing


Mermaid source




On entry, CameraSession::processRequest returns immediately if captureLimit_ is set and captureCount_ has already reached the limit. Otherwise, after optional sink_ processing and frame information output, captureCount_ is incremented. Afterward, when the configured captureLimit_ is reached, the function emits the captureDone signal and returns.

In this run, the callback (requestComplete) and the follow-up processing (processRequest) were observed on different threads, but this observation does not guarantee the thread placement of every run. The cancellation branch (Request::RequestCancelled) of requestComplete exists in the source, but it was not exercised dynamically in this run.


We reproduce after confirming a supported kernel and device access.



1. 


The developer investigates the Kconfig and dependent symbols of the selected kernel. The developer prioritizes investigation that does not change host state and does not affect the existing WSL/Docker.


2. 


The developer directly investigates which modules are actually usable on the selected kernel. The developer does not treat adding a new distribution to the existing WSL as isolating the shared kernel. UDMABUF is a built-in feature, not a module.


3. 


Based on the results of investigating kernel configuration, dependencies, actually usable modules, and allocator candidates, the developer selects one DMA-BUF allocator. To reproduce the run recorded in this document as-is, the udmabuf condition must be met. A test that selects a different allocator is distinguished from a same-condition reproduction of this recorded procedure.


4. 


If shared kernel, module, .wslconfig changes, or distribution or VM shutdown is required, you must write the impact, backup path, and recovery plan, and you must obtain separate approval immediately before executing the host change. PR merge or Pages deployment approval does not replace this host change approval. Docker is stopped only when the approved plan requires stopping it.


5. 


After receiving separate host change approval immediately before execution, the developer installs and boots the recorded test kernel by following the approved host change plan.


6. 


The developer checks the recorded test conditions against the actual state before execution. The running kernel version is checked against 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2-issue211, the permission to open /dev/udmabuf from the root child context is checked against the recorded state, and the camera configuration is checked against Virtual2, 400x300, and NV12. All three conditions must match, and if any one differs, capture is not performed.


7. 


If the conditions for execution are met, the developer runs the official cam debug build, not modified with observation tools, according to the linked reproduction guide.




From environment verification to reproduction execution


Mermaid source




